      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Defines the terms used in the Act. A 'young person' is a person who is, or appears to be, 12 years old or older but under 18, and includes anyone charged or found guilty under the Act for an offence committed while a young person. A 'child' is under 12. An 'adult sentence' is any sentence that could be imposed on an adult convicted of the same offence. The 'Attorney General' is defined as in s. 2 of the Criminal Code, extended to cover extrajudicial measures. A 'conference' is a group convened to give advice under s. 19, and 'extrajudicial measures' are measures other than judicial proceedings (including extrajudicial sanctions). A 'parent' includes anyone with a legal duty to provide for the young person or with legal or actual custody or control. The 'custodial portion' of a youth sentence under s. 42(2)(n), (o), (q) or (r) is the part served in custody before supervision in the community.",
