      summary: "Sets out the principles of the youth criminal justice system. It exists to protect the public by holding young persons accountable through measures proportionate to the seriousness of the offence and their degree of responsibility, promoting rehabilitation and reintegration, and referring them to community programs that address the circumstances underlying their offending. It must be separate from the adult system, based on diminished moral blameworthiness, and emphasize rehabilitation and reintegration, fair and proportionate accountability, enhanced procedural protection (including privacy), timely intervention, and promptness. Measures should reinforce respect for societal values, encourage repair of harm, be meaningful for the individual young person, and respect their needs and characteristics, with particular attention to Aboriginal and Black young persons. Young persons have rights in their own right, including to be heard, and victims should be treated with courtesy, compassion, fairness and respect.",
