      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Appeals for indictable offences (or offences the Attorney General elects to proceed with as indictable) follow Part XXI of the Criminal Code, and appeals for summary conviction offences follow Part XXVII. Where indictable and summary offences are tried or sentenced together, the appeal follows Part XXI. A finding of contempt under s. 15, and its sentence, can be appealed as if it were a conviction and a sentence on indictment; s. 10 of the Criminal Code applies to a conviction of a parent for contempt under s. 27(4). Orders under s. 72(1) or (1.1) and 76(1) can be appealed as part of the sentence and, unless the appeal court orders otherwise, are heard together. If no election is made on a hybrid offence, the Attorney General is deemed to have elected summary conviction. Where the youth justice court is a superior court, a summary conviction appeal goes to the court of appeal; if the Nunavut Court of Justice is acting as youth justice court, it goes to a judge of the Nunavut Court of Appeal, with a further appeal to the Nunavut Court of Appeal. An appeal to the Supreme Court of Canada from a court of appeal judgment on a finding of guilt or dismissal under the indictable appeal route needs leave. No appeal lies from a youth sentence under s. 59 (other than s. 59(10)) or under ss. 94 to 96.",
