      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "A youth justice court cannot commit a young person to custody unless (a) the young person committed a violent offence; (b) the young person was previously found guilty under s. 137 in relation to more than one sentence and, where sentencing for s. 145(2) to (5) of the Criminal Code or s. 137, caused harm or risk of harm to public safety; (c) the young person committed an indictable offence for which an adult would face more than two years and has a history showing a pattern of extrajudicial sanctions or findings of guilt; or (d) in exceptional cases, the aggravating circumstances of an indictable offence make a non-custodial sentence inconsistent with the purpose and principles in s. 38. Where (a) to (c) apply, the court must still consider all reasonable alternatives to custody raised at the sentencing hearing, including submissions on available alternatives, the likelihood of compliance and alternatives used for similar offences. Custody cannot be used as a substitute for child protection, mental health or other social measures, and before imposing custody the court must consider a pre-sentence report (which can be dispensed with on consent) and any sentencing proposal from the young person.",
