      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Before imposing a youth sentence, the court must consider conference recommendations, any pre-sentence report, representations by the parties and the young person's parents, and other relevant information. It then imposes one or more of the listed sanctions that are not inconsistent with each other: a reprimand; an absolute or conditional discharge; a fine of up to $1,000; compensation or restitution; personal or community service; an order of prohibition, seizure or forfeiture available under another Act; probation of up to two years; an intensive support and supervision program; a non-residential program (up to 240 hours over six months); a custody and supervision order (custody followed by supervision half as long, totalling up to two years, or three where the offence carries life imprisonment); custody under paragraph (o) for attempted murder, manslaughter or aggravated sexual assault (up to three years); a deferred custody and supervision order (up to six months); a sentence for first degree murder (up to ten years, up to six in custody) or second degree murder (up to seven years, up to four in custody); or an intensive rehabilitative custody and supervision order. For first or second degree murder within the meaning of s. 231 of the Criminal Code the court must impose the paragraph (q) or (r)(ii) or (iii) sanction. A youth sentence other than orders under paragraphs (j), (n), (o), (q) or (r) cannot last more than two years, and combined sentences for different offences cannot continuously exceed three years (with exceptions, including murder). A sentence comes into force when imposed or on a later date the court specifies, and custodial sentences may be ordered to be served consecutively.",
