      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "When a court makes an order under s. 42(2)(k) or (l), it must have the order read to or by the young person, explain its purpose and effect and confirm the young person understands, and give a copy to the young person and any parent present. It may also give a copy to a parent not present who is taking an active interest. The young person endorses an acknowledgement on the order, but failure to do so, or a parent not receiving a copy, does not affect validity. The order takes effect when made, or, if the sentence includes continuous custody and supervision, at the end of the supervision period, or, if there is a deferred custody and supervision order, at the end of that order's period. Where custody is delayed under s. 42(12), the court may split the order so the first portion runs until custody starts and the rest takes effect at the end of supervision. The young person can be given notice to appear, and if they fail to attend after written notice is proved, the court may issue a warrant.",
