      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "A youth justice court must review a youth sentence other than a custody and supervision order under s. 42(2)(n), (o), (q) or (r), on application of the young person, a parent, the Attorney General or the provincial director, if there are grounds: a material change in circumstances, the young person being unable or having serious difficulty complying, a breach without reasonable excuse of a s. 42(2)(k) or (l) condition, the terms adversely affecting access to services, education or employment, or any other ground the court considers appropriate. The court may require a progress report from the provincial director and compel the young person to appear. After hearing the young person, a parent, the Attorney General and the provincial director, it may confirm the sentence, terminate it and discharge the young person from further obligation, or vary it or impose a new youth sentence, other than custody, for no longer than the rest of the earlier sentence. Without the young person's consent, the new sentence cannot be more onerous than what remained, except that the court may extend time to comply with a fine, compensation, restitution or service order by up to 12 months, and may add or tighten conditions after a breach of a discharge condition.",
