      title: "Effect of absolute discharge or termination of youth sentence",
      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Subject to s. 12 of the Canada Evidence Act, when a young person is found guilty and receives an absolute discharge, or the youth sentence (or a Young Offenders Act disposition) has ceased to have effect, the young person is deemed not to have been found guilty or convicted, other than for a s. 51 prohibition order. Exceptions allow the young person to plead autrefois convict, a court to consider the finding on an adult sentence application, release or sentencing, and parole boards to consider it for conditional release or a record suspension. The discharge or end of sentence also removes any legal disqualification that would follow from the finding of guilt. Application forms for federal employment, Crown corporations, the Canadian Forces and federally regulated work cannot ask about such findings, and a finding of guilt under this Act is not a previous conviction for offences with greater punishment for repeat offenders, except when determining an adult sentence.",
