      title: "Levels of custody",
      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Each province's youth custody and supervision system must have at least two levels of custody distinguished by degree of restraint, and each facility with one or more levels is designated by the lieutenant governor in council (or a delegate for a single least-restrictive level). The provincial director determines the appropriate custody level when a young person is committed under s. 42(2)(n), (o), (q) or (r) and certain other orders, and may later set a different level when the young person's needs and society's interests would be better served. The level should be the least restrictive, having regard to the seriousness of the offence, the young person's needs and circumstances (including proximity to family, school, employment and support services), the safety of other young persons and society's interests, should best match programs to the young person's needs, and must consider the likelihood of escape. The young person and a parent get written notice with reasons.",
