      title: "Continuation of custody",
      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "For a sentence under s. 42(2)(o), (q) or (r), the Attorney General may apply, within a reasonable time before the custodial portion ends, for continued custody, and the provincial director must bring the young person before the court. After hearing both parties and a parent, the court may order the young person to stay in custody up to the rest of the sentence if there are reasonable grounds to believe the young person is likely to commit an offence causing the death of or serious harm to another person before the sentence expires. The court considers relevant factors, including a pattern of persistent violent behaviour, psychiatric or psychological evidence, and reliable information that the young person plans such an offence. If the hearing cannot finish in time, the court may keep the young person in custody until the application is decided.",
