      title: "Persons having access to records",
      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Sets out who may be given access to records from the date a record is created until the end of the access period in subsection (2). On request, the following must be given access to court records (s. 114) and may be given access to police and government records (ss. 115 and 116): the young person, their counsel, the Attorney General, the victim, a parent during proceedings or the youth sentence, an adult assisting the young person under s. 25(7), a peace officer for law enforcement or administrative purposes, a judge, court or review board, the provincial director or adult correctional director, people taking part in conferences or extrajudicial measures, ombudsmen and privacy or information commissioners investigating a complaint, coroners and child advocates, people acting under the Firearms Act, government staff carrying out duties under the Act or supervising the young person, people doing a required criminal record check for employment or services, an accused who swears access is needed for full answer and defence, and anyone a youth justice court judge finds has a valid interest for research, statistical or proper-administration-of-justice reasons. Access periods: two years after consent to an extrajudicial sanction; for an acquittal, two months after the appeal period (three months after an appeal ends); for a dismissal, withdrawal or a finding of guilt with a reprimand, two months after that event; one year after an absolute discharge; three years after a conditional discharge; three years after the youth sentence is completed for a summary conviction offence; five years for an indictable offence. Records of extrajudicial measures other than sanctions are available only to a limited group for limited purposes, and withheld reports and medical or psychological and DNA records have narrower access.",
