      summary: "After the access period in s. 119(2) has ended, a youth justice court judge may, on application, order that a person be given access to all or part of a record kept under ss. 114 to 116, or a copy, if satisfied that (a) the person has a valid and substantial interest, access is necessary in the interest of the proper administration of justice, and disclosure is not prohibited by another Act; or (b) access is desirable in the public interest for research or statistical purposes. Under (a), a record about a class of young persons can be sought only if the young persons cannot reasonably be identified and disclosure is necessary to investigate a suspected offence against a young person while serving a sentence. An application under (a) must not be heard unless the young person and the record holder were given at least five days' written notice and a chance to be heard; the judge may waive notice to the young person if notice would frustrate the application or reasonable efforts to find the young person failed. The order must set out the purposes for which the record may be used.",
