      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "A peace officer may disclose to any person information in a court record (s. 114) or police record (s. 115) that it is necessary to disclose in conducting an investigation. The Attorney General may, in a proceeding, disclose record information to a co-accused of the young person, and, to an accused, information identifying a witness as a young person dealt with under the Act. The Attorney General or a peace officer may also disclose information to the federal Minister of Justice as necessary for a request to or by a foreign state under the Mutual Legal Assistance in Criminal Matters Act or for extradition. A peace officer may disclose to an insurance company to investigate a claim arising from the offence. The provincial director or a youth worker may disclose information needed to obtain information for a report required by the Act. The provincial director, youth worker, Attorney General, peace officer or others providing services to young persons may disclose record information to people supervising or caring for the young person, including schools, if necessary to ensure the young person complies with an authorization under s. 91 or a youth court order, to ensure the safety of staff, students or others, or to help rehabilitate the young person. The recipient must keep it separate, limit access to it, and destroy their copy when no longer needed. No disclosure under this section may be made after the s. 119(2) access period ends.",
