      title: "Sections of Criminal Code applicable",
      severity: "",
      maxPenalty: "",
      summary: "Section 16 (mental disorder defence) and Part XX.1 (mental disorder) of the Criminal Code apply, with necessary modifications, to proceedings under this Act. Where Part XX.1 requires a copy to be given to an accused or party, it is also given to counsel for the young person, a parent attending the proceedings, and a parent not attending who is, in the court's or Review Board's opinion, taking an active interest. Where Part XX.1 requires notice to an accused or party, notice is also given to a parent and counsel. Failure to give a parent notice does not generally affect validity, but it invalidates later proceedings unless a parent attends with the young person or the judge or Review Board adjourns for notice to be given or dispenses with it. Before making or reviewing a Part XX.1 disposition, the court or Review Board must consider the young person's age and special needs and any representations by a parent. The two-year period in s. 672.33(1) of the Criminal Code (fitness to stand trial) is one year for young persons, and a hospital means one designated by the provincial Minister of Health for young persons.",
